  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 532 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSunday, the low over the Pacific Northwest starts to push\nagainst our upper level ridge as it tries to push further into\nthe interior of western United States. When this occurs more of\nthe monsoonal moisture will feed over our region priming the\nIntermountain west for more storm activity. The storm coverage\nwill slowly increase as well as more moisture feeds into the\nregion. The system will inch towards the Intermountain West and\nstart to flatten our ridge. \n\nThe trend of showers and thunderstorms continue through Monday\nand Tuesday as the stubborn trough to the northwest finally\nejects into the central plains. While moisture will remain\nhigh, a lobe of drier air associated with a ridge to the east\nmay be pulled in ahead of the trough which would reduce rain\npotential. However, since the trough is ejecting it is possible\nthat cyclogenesis occurs nearby increasing the severe weather\npotential. Some machine learning models are highlighting an\nuptick in that potential for the Nebraska Panhandle area.\nThe details of how this unfolds will be ironed out as we get\ncloser.\n\nThe remainder of the long term is in a drying trend. The\nridge noses in. This would not completely shut down storm\nactivity.",
